---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import counterSrc from "../../examples/counter.rs?raw";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---
<Base title="Overview" current="home">
  <div class="hero-wrap">
    <!-- QuEra atom cloud: overlapping spheres filled with the dawn
         linear-gradient, blurred and concentrated in the upper-right via a
         radial mask so the reading column on the left stays clean. -->
    <svg class="atom-cloud" viewBox="0 0 1600 700" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice" aria-hidden="true">
      <defs>
        <linearGradient id="quera-dawn" x1="0" y1="0" x2="1" y2="1">
          <stop offset="0%"  stop-color="#7f3eff"/>
          <stop offset="50%" stop-color="#fa82ec"/>
          <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#ff7c24"/>
        </linearGradient>
        <radialGradient id="cloud-mask" cx="78%" cy="35%" r="55%">
          <stop offset="0%"  stop-color="#fff" stop-opacity="1"/>
          <stop offset="55%" stop-color="#fff" stop-opacity="0.6"/>
          <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#fff" stop-opacity="0"/>
        </radialGradient>
        <mask id="cloud-fade">
          <rect x="0" y="0" width="1600" height="700" fill="url(#cloud-mask)"/>
        </mask>
        <filter id="cloud-blur" x="-30%" y="-30%" width="160%" height="160%">
          <feGaussianBlur stdDeviation="14"/>
        </filter>
      </defs>
      <g class="cloud-atoms" fill="url(#quera-dawn)" mask="url(#cloud-fade)" filter="url(#cloud-blur)">
        <circle cx="900"  cy="120" r="120"/>
        <circle cx="1060" cy="230" r="160"/>
        <circle cx="1240" cy="140" r="110"/>
        <circle cx="1380" cy="260" r="140"/>
        <circle cx="1500" cy="160" r="100"/>
        <circle cx="980"  cy="360" r="130"/>
        <circle cx="1180" cy="400" r="150"/>
        <circle cx="1360" cy="450" r="110"/>
        <circle cx="1520" cy="350" r="90"/>
        <circle cx="780"  cy="220" r="90"/>
        <circle cx="820"  cy="480" r="100"/>
      </g>
    </svg>

    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <p class="eyebrow">QuEra Computing · Rust</p>
        <h1>vihaco</h1>
        <p class="byline">
          A virtual ISA and machine framework for orchestrating hybrid
          analog/digital quantum control. Define instruction sets, components,
          effects, and parsers as ordinary Rust — then compose them into a
          machine.
        </p>
        <div class="meta">
          <span><strong>Rust</strong> 2024</span>
          <span><strong>License</strong> MIT</span>
          <span><strong>Source</strong> <a href="https://github.com/QuEraComputing/vihaco">github.com/QuEraComputing/vihaco</a></span>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href={`${base}/quickstart`}>Quick Start</a>
          <a class="btn" href={`${base}/guide`}>Guides</a>
          <a class="btn" href={`${base}/reference`}>API Reference</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="hero-code">
        <figcaption>
          <span>A component</span>
          <span class="hero-code-file"><code>counter.rs</code></span>
        </figcaption>
        <pre><code class="language-rust">{counterSrc}</code></pre>
        <p class="hero-code-note">
          A component is an execution unit: it takes a typed instruction plus
          a resolved message and returns a typed result. Dialects declare
          the vocabulary; composites supply execution policy.
        </p>
      </figure>
    </section>
  </div>

  <article class="shell home">
    <section class="triptych" aria-label="What vihaco is for">
      <div class="panel">
        <span class="num">i. Instruction sets</span>
        <h2>A vocabulary shared by devices</h2>
        <p>
          <code>#[dialect]</code> defines typed instructions. Components declare
          whole dialects and implement their operations; composites route those
          operations to direct or nested devices.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <span class="num">ii. Components &amp; effects</span>
        <h2>Execute and handle results</h2>
        <p>
          Components execute through <code>Execute</code>. Authored steps prepare
          messages, call devices, and apply results. Each machine makes that
          continuation policy explicit.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <span class="num">iii. Pattern-derived syntax</span>
        <h2>From source text to module</h2>
        <p>
          <code>#[derive(Parse)]</code> generates a
          <a href="https://github.com/zesterer/chumsky">chumsky</a> parser for
          your instruction syntax from declarative, compile-time-checked
          patterns. Parsing and symbolic lowering build a module, which an
          image owns beside its mutable execution machine.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="install-tabs" aria-label="Install vihaco">
      <h2 class="section-h"><span class="section-h-num">§</span>Install</h2>
      <p>
        <code>vihaco</code> is the facade for declarations and execution.
        The <a href={`${base}/guide`}>author’s guide setup</a> selects the version
        and dependencies used throughout the examples.
      </p>
      <p class="install-pane-note">Requires Rust edition 2024.</p>
      <a class="install-pane-link" href={`${base}/quickstart`}>Continue to the Quick Start →</a>
    </section>

    <p class="home-foot">
      Continue to the
      <a href={`${base}/quickstart`}>Quick Start</a>,
      work through the <a href={`${base}/guide`}>Guides</a>,
      or browse the <a href={`${base}/reference`}>API Reference</a>.
    </p>
  </article>

</Base>
